          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "Thank you for telling me. Coronary artery disease is a condition you may want to understand well, and I can explain what the guidance says.\n\n**What it means:** According to the General clinical reference on coronary artery disease, it is the build-up of plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle. This builds up over many years. It can narrow the arteries, which limits blood flow when the heart needs more, often during exertion. It can also cause a plaque to rupture, which may lead to a sudden blockage. That is why having no symptoms in the past does not rule out a heart attack now.\n\n**General lifestyle guidance:** The retrieved passages on diet and activity mostly cover high blood pressure and heart failure, not coronary artery disease directly. They describe these general points, which your own clinician can tell you how to apply to you:\n- Reducing salt and reading food labels for sodium.\n- Eating more vegetables, fruit, beans and dairy for potassium, and checking first if you have kidney disease or take medicines that affect potassium.\n- Limiting alcohol.\n- Building up physical activity gradually, with an assessment first if you have known heart disease.\n\n**Important:** If you have chest pain, pressure or tightness, breathlessness out of proportion to effort, fainting, or palpitations, seek urgent medical care now. Do not wait for an appointment.\n\nWhat would you like to know next? I can cover symptoms, medications in general terms, or diet and activity in more detail.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
