          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which parts of that are lifestyle rather than medication?",
          "answer": "The earlier discussion covered secondary prevention for coronary artery disease, and it named two medication measures. I also need to correct something I said before: I described the secondary-prevention passage as cut off, but the retrieved text ends at the statin item. It does not list the non-drug measures. It only says they are often the ones dropped.\n\n**Medication (from the secondary-prevention guidance):**\n- **Antiplatelet therapy**, which helps stop blood clots. Most people take one long term, and some take two for a defined period after a heart attack or stent. The guidance says the duration is set against bleeding risk.\n- **High-intensity statin therapy**, which lowers cholesterol.\n\n**Lifestyle (not listed as secondary-prevention components in the retrieved text):**\nThe coronary artery disease reference names factors that contribute to the disease and can be changed: smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber, and chronic kidney disease. Some of these also appear in the blood-pressure guidance, which lists reducing salt, eating more vegetables, fruit and whole grains, regular aerobic activity, weight reduction, limiting alcohol and stopping smoking. That guidance is about blood pressure, so the corpus does not confirm how far it applies to coronary artery disease.\n\nThe corpus does not give a full non-drug plan for coronary artery disease. Your clinician can tell you which of these measures apply to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
